==fq-02==
4:2. verdict: supported
5:3. investigated_claim: The splitter defect (split_task removes original task ids without rewiring downstream depends_on/dependency_reasons) is local to the finalize step; no production code outside finalize calls split_task/split_high_complexity_tasks/_split_finalize_tasks, and no runtime consumer assumes original IDs survive — so the defect requires a fix scoped to finalize, with the caveat that the fix surface inside finalize extends beyond dependency rewiring to coverage/custody and plan-steps metadata.
152:9. adherence_classification: ADHERENCE
153:10. missing_or_contradictory_structure: none for the primary split→feasibility contract (identity-matched producer/consumer/persistence/policy trace complete). Secondary note (not a contract gap): no test exercises a downstream task referencing a split id, and no test references _split_finalize_tasks directly.
165:15. immediate_decision_informed: The deterministic finalize blocker is local to finalize in call-site terms: no other production component invokes the splitter, so the repair can be scoped to the engine runtime without compatibility shims for external callers. However, the minimal fix must be a coordinated rewire (map original_id → impl_id in downstream depends_on/dependency_reasons AND in critique_resolution_coverage rows; optionally remap plan_steps_covered finalize_item_ids/orphan reconciliation), otherwise finalize can still fail at custody bind (different fingerprint) after dependency rewiring; regression tests must add a downstream-references-split-task case (splitter unit + M8A finalize wiring + feasibility + critique custody).
166:16. durable_decision_informed: The splitter's contract is "original task ids are replaced and must be treated as non-surviving inside finalize"; the splitter is a run_authority-owned orchestrate boundary per M10 inventory, so any API change to split_task/split_high_complexity_tasks signature or id-mapping semantics must be reflected in the boundary inventory (m10-supported-boundaries.json, wbc-boundary-inventory.json) and in the idempotent double-call design (2059/2135); task_contract_hash sensitivity to depends_on means rewiring changes admission identity — acceptable since publication is CAS-gated on the plan lock and no admitted artifact exists for this plan.
168:18. unresolved_questions:
==fq-03==
4:2. verdict: supported
5:3. investigated_claim: The exact fix boundary for the R7 splitter rewire is depends_on/dependency_reasons only; every other field that stores task IDs (critique_resolution_coverage, validation coverage, user-action fields) is either validated downstream such that split IDs require normalization there, or not validated at all — i.e., coverage/verification mutations add a second contract defect.
184:9. adherence_classification: MISSING_STRUCTURE
185:10. missing_or_contradictory_structure: The task-ID reference integrity contract outside depends_on/dependency_reasons has no complete edge: (a) validation.plan_steps_covered[].finalize_item_ids — producer (pre-split handler coverage) → consumer (feasibility/bind) has NO validation edge at all; stale pre-split ids persist into published artifacts. (b) critique_resolution_coverage[].task_ids — producer (model, pre-split) → consumer (post-split bind validation) is VALIDATED but against a mutated task set with no normalization step between, so the contract is contradictory across the two call sites (passes at 469, fails at 2164). The splitter mutation (task_splitter.py) is the un-accounted producer that invalidates both.
202:15. immediate_decision_informed: Exact fix boundary for the R7 superfixer: the splitter rewire must normalize, in addition to downstream depends_on/dependency_reasons (original_id → impl_id), BOTH (a) critique_resolution_coverage[*].task_ids (original_id → impl_id) — otherwise bind_finalize_custody raises finalize_critique_coverage_invalid after task_feasibility.json is written but before finalize.json is published (partial-write state at the publication boundary); and (b) validation.plan_steps_covered[*].finalize_item_ids (original_id → impl_id) — otherwise stale pre-split ids persist into the published candidate and orphan_tasks is inflated. Verification mutation adds no defect; user-action gate mutation adds no defect (depends_on-only). Feasibility is not the right place to re-validate coverage (it is deliberately scoped to the task graph); the bind-time validator is the enforcing consumer, so normalization must occur in the splitter (or equivalently re-derive coverage post-split before bind).
203:16. durable_decision_informed: The splitter is a shared mutation primitive whose contract ("removes an id") is unsafe for any external reference; a durable invariant is that post-split graphs must be reference-closed across ALL ID-bearing fields (depends_on, dependency_reasons, critique_resolution_coverage.task_ids, validation.plan_steps_covered.finalize_item_ids, user_actions.blocks_task_ids, stances.task_id), with a single normalization pass and a reference-closure assertion at the persistence boundary (before task_feasibility.json write at finalize.py:2156) rather than a late bind-time rejection after the admission write. Also consider reordering: run coverage derivation AFTER split (or re-derive it) so plan_steps_covered is born post-split.
205:18. unresolved_questions:

